    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d300">

                <pb facs="0427" n="419" xml:id="pg419"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.94/2344<hi rendition="#superscript">2/25</hi>
                </note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Adviser on Political Relations</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Hornbeck</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-09-02">September 2, 1941</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>There are certain points that we should keep vividly in mind, among them
                    these:</p>
                <p>Japan is in a weakened and a perilous position;</p>
                <p>Japanese leaders are contending among themselves and are uncertain and
                    fearful;</p>
                <p>Japan is in no position to attack, with expectation of success, either the
                    Russians, the British and Dutch or the United States;</p>
                <p>It is the Japanese who are eager for and who are asking for this conference;</p>
                <p>This approach, by one element in Japan’s leadership, is a confession of internal
                    weakness and external weakness;</p>
                <p>The real “crisis” is in Japan.</p>
                <p>Political confusion within Japan has its potential advantages as well as its
                    potential dangers.</p>
                <p>Although we should take no unfair advantage, we have everything to gain and
                    little or nothing to lose by standing firm on our principles and our
                    policies.</p>
            </div>
